{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789241985,"nonce":"pztPWSYfUi1Br8RhqK+VD/w2ELM=","title":"Dicts and sets are linear scans over an ordered list","message":"A dict is a `List\u003cAny\u003e` of alternating keys and values, a set a\n`List\u003cAny\u003e` of distinct values, each boxed under its own tag. Lookup,\ninsertion and membership compare with the dynamic equality one pair at\na time, so every operation is O(n) in the container's size. Iteration\norder is insertion order, as Python's is.\n\nA hash index alongside the list (hash of the boxed value, open\naddressing over pair positions) would bring lookup to O(1) without\nchanging the representation the rest of the library sees; `zb_any_hash`\ndoes not exist yet, and a dict literal with duplicate keys is resolved\nby `zb_dict_from_pairs` at run time rather than at compile time.","files":null}]}